          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What sort of dietary change does guidance describe for high blood pressure?",
          "answer": "Guidance describes two main kinds of dietary change for high blood pressure: cutting salt and eating a particular pattern of food. Alcohol limits are also part of the advice.\n\n**What the guidance recommends**\n\n- **Less sodium.** A major cardiology society says every major body recommends reducing dietary sodium (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment). [removed: this system does not give doses or amounts.] It notes that most sodium comes from packaged and restaurant food, not the salt shaker (lifestyle-hypertension-diet).\n- **A \"whole-diet\" pattern.** The cardiology guidance recommends foods rich in vegetables, fruit, whole grains and low-fat dairy, and low in saturated fat. The general reference describes this as the approach with the most evidence. It adds poultry, fish, beans, nuts and vegetable oils, and it limits fatty meat, full-fat dairy, sweets and sugary drinks. It is a whole pattern, not a single \"blood pressure food.\"\n- **More potassium, through food.** Higher potassium intake is linked to lower blood pressure. The reference describes getting it from food rather than supplements. It warns that potassium-based salt substitutes are not suitable for everyone, especially people with kidney disease or on medicines that raise potassium. That decision belongs with the person's own clinician.\n- **Less alcohol.** Alcohol raises blood pressure in a dose-related way. Commonly stated limits are up to two drinks a day for men and one for women, with less being better (lifestyle-hypertension-diet).\n\n**How strong the evidence is**\n\nThe retrieved passages say sodium reduction is the most-studied dietary change for blood pressure, that the effect is graded, and that combining the eating pattern with sodium reduction works better than either alone. They do not grade the evidence formally, so I cannot give a formal strength rating from these sources.\n\n**Where authorities differ**\n\nThe retrieved sources do not show a disagreement about diet itself. The disagreements they describe concern blood pressure thresholds. US guidance calls 130/80 mm Hg the start of stage 1 hypertension, while European guidance uses 140/90 mm Hg and treats 130–139 / 80–89 as high-normal. Under European guidance, many people US guidance would class as stage 1 are advised on lifestyle changes alone (guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets; guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment).\n\nThe sodium targets and alcohol limits came from a general reference, not from a named guideline. I could not check whether individual authorities publish different numbers.\n\nA clinician or dietitian can help a person work out which changes suit them, especially if they have kidney disease or take medicines that affect potassium.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
